This is the auth + role-based authorization system I just built for you. Here's what each
 piece does:

The big picture: a visitor can register and log in (authentication), and once logged in, 
the app checks their role field to decide whether they're allowed onto certain pages 
(authorization). Regular users see a dashboard; only users with role = admin can reach /admin.

File by file:

2026_07_27_010000_add_role_to_users_table.php (migration) — adds a role column to 
Laravel's built-in users table, defaulting every new user to 'user'.
User.php (model) — represents a row in that users table. The key line is 'password' => 'hashed'
 in the casts() method: whenever you save a password to this model, Laravel automatically
  hashes it for you. isAdmin() is a small helper that returns true if role === 'admin'.
AuthController.php — handles the three core actions:
register() — validates the signup form, creates a User row (password gets hashed automatically 
by the cast above), then logs them in immediately.
login() — validates email/password, calls Auth::attempt() which checks the password against 
the hashed one in the database. If it matches, it starts a session; if not, it sends back an error.
logout() — ends the session.
CheckRole.php (middleware) — a gatekeeper function that runs before certain routes. It checks 
$request->user()->role !== $role (the role passed in, e.g. 'admin') and blocks access with a
 403 error if it doesn't match.
web.php (routes) — wires it together into three tiers:
guest middleware group → /register, /login (only visible to people not logged in)
auth middleware group → /dashboard, /logout (only visible if logged in — this is the 
authentication check)
role:admin middleware (nested inside auth) → /admin (only visible if logged in and role is
 admin — this is the authorization check)
The four .blade.php files — the actual HTML pages: registration form, login form, a dashboard 
showing the user's name/role (with an "Admin Page" button only if they're an admin), and the
 admin-only page itself.

So the flow is: register/login → authenticated session created → route middleware checks 
session exists (auth) → nested middleware checks role on top of that (role:admin). 
